</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>Corresponding author: <span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ling@ist.hokudai.ac.jp</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ling Xiao<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Hokkaido University, Sapporo, Japan
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Social robot navigation requires a sophisticated integration of scene semantics and human social norms. Scaling up Vision Language Models (VLMs) generally improves reasoning and decision-making capabilities for socially compliant navigation. However, increased model size incurs substantial computational overhead, limiting suitability for real-time robotic deployment. Conversely, lightweight VLMs enable efficient inference but often exhibit weaker reasoning and decision-making performance in socially complex environments. Achieving both strong reasoning ability and efficiency remains an open challenge. To bridge this gap, we propose Group Competitive Learning (GCL), a strategy designed to amplify the capabilities of lightweight VLMs. Our strategy introduces the Group Competitive Objective (GCO) to harmonize global semantics with distributional regularization, alongside Asymmetric Group Optimization (AGO) to explore the upper limits of model performance.
Empirical evaluations on social navigation benchmarks demonstrate that GCL significantly elevates VLM performance. Specifically, GCL enables the Qwen2.5-VL-3B learner model and guide Qwen3-VL-4B to achieve an F1 score of 0.968 and 0.914, representing 40% and 12% improvement over vanilla supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Notably, under vanilla SFT, the 3B model initially trails the 8B model (F1: 0.692 vs. 0.755). However, through the GCL, the 3B model outperforms (28%) the 8B baseline model. These results suggest that GCL provides an effective solution for achieving both high accuracy and computational efficiency in real-world deployment.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Social robot navigation small vision language models competitive learning human robot interaction
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Social robot navigation is a fundamental challenge in embodied AI, requiring agents to not only perceive complex scene semantics but also strictly adhere to human social norms to generate compliant action commands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. The emergence of Vision Language Models (VLMs) offers a promising paradigm for enabling robots to reason about visual observations through semantic and linguistic grounding. By aligning perception with high-level language representations, VLMs facilitate more informed and socially aware navigation behaviors.
Generally, increasing the scale of VLMs improves their reasoning and decision-making capabilities, which is particularly beneficial for socially compliant navigation tasks. However, larger models incur substantial computational cost, limiting their deployment in real-time robotic systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Conversely, lightweight VLMs enable efficient inference but often struggle to capture the complex social cues required for robust navigation in crowded or dynamic environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Achieving a balance between strong reasoning capability and computational efficiency therefore remains a key challenge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge the gap between VLM performance and efficiency, Knowledge Distillation (KD) has been the dominant paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Certain KD approaches focus on compressing the visual encoder or aligning cross-modal embeddings. Because, operating primarily at the visual feature level, these methods inadvertently neglect high-level reasoning consistency and specific decision-making processes, which are critical capabilities for social robot navigation tasks. Conversely, other techniques target the language model of VLMs by conducting distillation over the token-level output distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Although these strategies improve the efficiency of knowledge transfer between models, them lack explicit coordination between global semantic reasoning and token-level behaviors. Consequently, directly leveraging these KD strategies to train lightweight VLMs remains fundamentally infeasible in practical social robotics scenarios constrained by severely limited onboard memory and restricted task-specific dataset sizes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper aims to address these challenges. Specifically, we propose Group Competitive Learning (GCL), a novel strategy that enhances VLMs through group based competitive optimization, enabling effective knowledge transfer among models with similar or heterogeneous architectures without being constrained by model capacity. Our approach introduces three key contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose Group Competitive Learning (GCL), a training strategy that can be seamlessly integrated into various VLMs. GCL improves socially compliant reasoning and action generation across both homogeneous and heterogeneous architectures, regardless of model capacity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a Group Competitive Objective (GCO) to align VLMs at both semantic and token distribution levels through semantic consistency and distributional regularization. We further propose Asymmetric Group Optimization (AGO) to reveal the optimization dynamics and performance boundaries of GCL while avoiding exhaustive hyperparameter search.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments on two social navigation benchmarks show clear improvements over supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Under GCL, Qwen2.5-VL-3B improves by <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">40%</span> (F1: 0.968) and Qwen3-VL-4B by <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12%</span>. Notably, although the base 3B model initially lags behind the 8B counterpart (0.692 vs. 0.755), after GCL it surpasses the 8B baseline with a 28% improvement.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>VLMs in Social Robot Navigation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In human environments, social robot navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> refers to the robot’s ability to perceive its surroundings, interpret high-level semantic cues (e.g., human activities, spatial arrangements, social norms), and adjust its behavior in accordance with unwritten social conventions, such as maintaining personal space, yielding right-of-way, or avoiding collision trajectories through crowds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Unlike traditional reactive or rule-based navigation, social robot navigation leverages multimodal perception and semantic reasoning to generate socially compliant motion actions that are both safe and natural from a human perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. This paradigm is particularly critical for service and assistive robots operating in dynamic, unstructured settings like hospitals, shopping malls, or homes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While large VLMs offer strong perception and decision-making capabilities in human robot interaction tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, these models incur substantial computational overhead, limiting their deployment on resource constrained robotic platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Consequently, there remains a critical challenge in exploring efficient, lightweight VLMs that retain sufficient reasoning capacity for social robot navigation tasks without sacrificing deployability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Knowledge Distillation in VLM</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The deployment of VLMs is often bottlenecked by their immense computational footprints. To address this, Knowledge Distillation (KD) has become a cornerstone for transferring capabilities to compact students <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Knowledge distillation encompasses two principal paradigms: online distillation and offline distillation. Certain approaches compress visual encoders or align cross-modal embeddings in VLMs, evaluated on image centric tasks (e.g., captioning, VQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. These methods operate predominantly at the visual feature level and neglect high-level reasoning consistency and embodied decision-making, critical capabilities for socially compliant robot navigation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several methods focus on matching in language models of VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Shi et al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> augment the student model’s reasoning capabilities by distilling the teacher model’s chain-of-thought (CoT) outputs into experiential knowledge. Nevertheless, it remains inherently contingent upon both the teacher model’s quality and the precision of token-level alignment. Fan et al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> jointly optimize multiple models via prediction consistency using symmetric divergence losses and uniform schedules, showing efficacy in classification and language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. However, such strategies lack explicit coordination of global semantic reasoning and fine-grained token-level behaviors. When applied directly to VLMs with disparate capabilities and capacities, they often induce training instability or collapse, underscoring the need for capacity alignment mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview of Group Competitive Learning Strategy</h3>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.11447v1/method.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/182;" width="476" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">The Overview of GCL.<span id="S3.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The left of the figure illustrates the GCL strategy’s inputs examples: texts and images across social navigation dataset. The middle shows the GCL strategy’s components: Group Competitive Objective, to align VLMs at both semantic and distributional token levels, and Asymmetric Group Optimization, to explore the optimization dynamics and performance boundaries of GCL. The right shows the socially compliant instruction of GCL outputs.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing VLM knowledge distillation approaches prioritize visual level alignment for image centric tasks. Directly adopting existing strategy tends to be oblivious to the high-level reasoning and embodied decision-making essential for socially compliant navigation. To address this, we propose the Group Competitive Learning (GCL), as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Overview of Group Competitive Learning Strategy ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Enhancing Lightweight Vision Language Models through Group Competitive Learning for Socially Compliant Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, aiming to construct a dynamic, dual stream synergistic strategy where two VLMs with distinct architectures and parameter scales can mutually enhance each other through benign competition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a multimodal dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{v},x_{t},y)\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{v},x_{t},y)\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>: visual input, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: text input, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>: the target), GCL defines a competitive group <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=\{A(\theta_{A}),B(\theta_{B})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=\{A(\theta_{A}),B(\theta_{B})\}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>: parameters of competitors models <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>). Coordinated interactions across feature-level semantic and token-level distributions, GCL maximizes the posterior <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(y|x_{v},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(y|x_{v},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> while minimizing model semantic and generative discrepancies, thereby unlocking the group’s collective potential. Specifically, GCL consists of two components: Group Competitive Objective (GCO) loss and Asymmetric Group Optimization (AGO). GCO promotes semantic and distributional token level alignment. AGO helps unleash the whole potential of GCL by matching the capability and capacity gap between the competitive group.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Group Competitive Objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve comprehensive competition ranging from global semantic consistency to local generative precision, we design a loss function of Group Competitive Objective (GCO), <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{GCO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{GCO}</annotation></semantics></math>, comprising three components:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{GCO}=\lambda_{sup}\mathcal{L}_{sup}+\lambda_{GSL}\mathcal{L}_{GSL}+\lambda_{DRL}\mathcal{L}_{DRL}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{GCO}=\lambda_{sup}\mathcal{L}_{sup}+\lambda_{GSL}\mathcal{L}_{GSL}+\lambda_{DRL}\mathcal{L}_{DRL},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{sup}=1.0,\lambda_{GSL}=0.5,\text{and }\lambda_{DRL}=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{sup}=1.0,\lambda_{GSL}=0.5,\text{and }\lambda_{DRL}=0.4</annotation></semantics></math> are the weights of each loss term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervised Loss.</span>
To preserve instruction following capabilities and maintain alignment with the task distribution throughout competitive training, we retain the autoregressive language modeling loss. For any model <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{A,B\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{A,B\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the group, the loss is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{sup}^{(k)}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\log P(y_{n}|y_{&lt;n},x_{v},x_{t};\theta_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{sup}^{(k)}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\log P(y_{n}|y_{&lt;n},x_{v},x_{t};\theta_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th step, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> represents the total number of steps. The total supervised loss is <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{sup}=\mathcal{L}_{sup}^{(A)}+\mathcal{L}_{sup}^{(B)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{sup}=\mathcal{L}_{sup}^{(A)}+\mathcal{L}_{sup}^{(B)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Global Semantic Loss (GSL).</span>
Existing online distillation approaches often neglect misalignment between different models when encoding high-level semantics feature. To address this, we propose the Global Semantic Loss (GSL). Since VLMs output variable length hidden state sequences <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, direct alignment is infeasible. GSL introduces a learnable attention pooling module to aggregate <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A},H_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A},H_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> into a compact global semantic vector <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{A},z_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{A},z_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subsequently, we project the vectors into a common feature space with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L2</annotation></semantics></math> normalization to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}_{A},\bar{z}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}_{A},\bar{z}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we employ an InfoNCE-style contrastive loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>
to maximize the semantic consistency of the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th inputs across different models:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{GSL}=-\frac{1}{B_{size}}\sum_{i=1}^{B_{size}}\log\frac{\exp(\text{sim}(\bar{z}_{A,i},\bar{z}_{B,i})/\tau)}{\sum_{j=1}^{B_{size}}\exp(\text{sim}(\bar{z}_{A,i},\bar{z}_{B,j})/\tau)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{GSL}=-\frac{1}{B_{size}}\sum_{i=1}^{B_{size}}\log\frac{\exp(\text{sim}(\bar{z}_{A,i},\bar{z}_{B,i})/\tau)}{\sum_{j=1}^{B_{size}}\exp(\text{sim}(\bar{z}_{A,i},\bar{z}_{B,j})/\tau)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike pretraining, which demands extensive negative samples for representation learning from scratch, GSL operates during fine tuning and remains effective under memory constrained regimes with small global batch sizes (16). In GCL, GSL acts as a semantic direction guide, aligning the optimization direction of models with mature representations in feature space to enable high dimensional semantic competition and consensus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distributional Regularization Loss (DRL).</span>
To address the sparsity of GSL’s feature-level alignment signals, we propose the Distributional Regularization Loss (DRL), a dense and batch independent regularization term operating directly on full vocabulary probability distributions over <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. We employ the Jensen-Shannon divergence to constrain the distributions of both models:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{DRL}=\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{A}||\mathcal{M})/2+\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{B}||\mathcal{M})/2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{DRL}=\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{A}||\mathcal{M})/2+\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{B}||\mathcal{M})/2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{A},P_{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{A},P_{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the full softmax probability of the models, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=1/2(P_{A}+P_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=1/2(P_{A}+P_{B})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the average distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As a soft constraint, DRL leverages token-level competition to transfer implicit knowledge through non-target token distributions, mitigating catastrophic degradation during fine-tuning and enhancing generalization to unseen distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Asymmetric Group Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To resolve optimization dynamics mismatch and efficiently characterize GCL performance boundaries, we introduce Asymmetric Group Optimization (AGO), which decouples the joint parameter space into independent subspaces (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>) for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of models A and B, assigning distinct learning rates <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and temperatures <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> per model at step <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}^{t+1}\leftarrow\text{Optimizer}(\nabla\mathcal{L}_{GCO},\eta_{k}(t),\tau_{k}),\quad k\in\{A,B\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Optimizer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}^{t+1}\leftarrow\text{Optimizer}(\nabla\mathcal{L}_{GCO},\eta_{k}(t),\tau_{k}),\quad k\in\{A,B\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta{k}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta{k}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the updated parameters of model <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Optimizer}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Optimizer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Optimizer}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the parameter update function, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathcal{L}_{GCO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathcal{L}_{GCO}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gradient of the GCO loss, while <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}(t)\neq\eta_{rival}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}(t)\neq\eta_{rival}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}\neq\tau_{rival}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}\neq\tau_{rival}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the model specific hyperparameters. Specifically, AGO introduces a dual assignment rule:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance based Role Assignment (Learning Rate).</span> <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> assignment is determined by SFT performance: the better performance model serves as the <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Guide</span>, assigned a lower learning rate (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{low}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{low}</annotation></semantics></math>) to preserve pretrained normative knowledge stability; the worse performance model functions as the <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learner</span>, receiving a higher learning rate (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{high}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{high}</annotation></semantics></math>) to enhance plasticity for rapid alignment to ground truth (GT) and the Guide’s semantic space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Capacity based Entropy Control (Temperature).</span> Temperature assignment is governed exclusively by parameter size, independent of model role. Larger models adopt <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{low}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{low}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve discriminative feature boundaries and prevent entropy collapse; smaller models adopt <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{high}=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{high}=3.0</annotation></semantics></math> to smooth output distributions and enhance plasticity for capturing “dark knowledge”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To rigorously demonstrate how this capacity based temperature scaling governs the learning dynamics, we analyze the gradient of our customized <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{DRL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{DRL}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k},(k\in{A,B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k},(k\in{A,B})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the softened output probability distribution of model <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The normalized loss after adding the asymmetric temperature coefficient is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{DRL}=\tau_{A}^{2}\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{A}||\mathcal{M})/2+\tau_{B}^{2}\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{B}||\mathcal{M})/2" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{DRL}=\tau_{A}^{2}\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{A}||\mathcal{M})/2+\tau_{B}^{2}\mathcal{D}_{KL}(P_{B}||\mathcal{M})/2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(P_{A}+P_{B})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(P_{A}+P_{B})/2</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the mean distribution. By applying the chain rule with the temperature scaled softmax Jacobian, the gradient with respect to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k,i}</annotation></semantics></math> (the previous softmax logit of the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th class for model <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) reveals a profound asymmetry:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{L}_{DRL}/\partial z_{k,i}\propto\underbrace{\tau_{k}}_{\textbf{Basic Alignment}}-\underbrace{\left(\tau_{rival}^{2}-\tau_{k}^{2}\right)}_{\textbf{Asymmetric Shift Force}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Basic Alignment</mtext></munder><mo>−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Asymmetric Shift Force</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{L}_{DRL}/\partial z_{k,i}\propto\underbrace{\tau_{k}}_{\textbf{Basic Alignment}}-\underbrace{\left(\tau_{rival}^{2}-\tau_{k}^{2}\right)}_{\textbf{Asymmetric Shift Force}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">details provided in the Supplementary Material</span> where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{rival}\in{\tau_{A},\tau_{B}}\setminus{\tau_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{rival}\in{\tau_{A},\tau_{B}}\setminus{\tau_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the temperature of the other model in the competitive group. This mathematical formulation explicitly proves why temperature must be coupled with model size: The <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> term exponentially amplifies the gradient stiffness of larger models (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>), allowing them to act as rigid semantic anchors regardless of whether they are learning or guiding. Meanwhile, the discrepancy term <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{rival}^{2}-\tau_{k}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{rival}^{2}-\tau_{k}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> injects a an attractive force for the larger model(<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>) and dynamic repulsive force for the smaller model (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=3.0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Through this AGO, the GCL strategy mathematically guarantees optimal synergistic gain, accommodating complex competitive groups (e.g., a smaller guide grouped with a larger learner) within a unified joint training process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Evaluation Metrics.</span> We conduct experiments using SNEI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> (265/60 train/test social navigation scenarios, 1,625 image-text pairs) and MUSON <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> (640/160 scenarios, 4,000 pairs) to evaluate performance and generalization. To provide a holistic assessment of the generated response quality, we employ a diverse set of semantic metrics:
we report Action-F1 score. Specifically, given an output action <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and a ground truth <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, a pretrained BERT encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> produces token embeddings <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{e}^{1},\ldots,y_{e}^{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{e}^{1},\ldots,y_{e}^{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{e}^{1},\ldots,g_{e}^{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{e}^{1},\ldots,g_{e}^{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of tokens in the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
We calculate the cosine similarity <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cosine}(y_{e}^{j},g_{e}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cosine</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cosine}(y_{e}^{j},g_{e}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> between token pairs using the results of embedding.
Based on the cosine similarity, recall <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and precision <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are computed as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R=\frac{1}{m}\sum\nolimits_{k=1}^{m}\max_{1\leq j\leq n}\mathrm{cosine}(y_{e}^{j},g_{e}^{k}),\quad P=\frac{1}{n}\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{n}\max_{1\leq k\leq m}\mathrm{cosine}(y_{e}^{j},g_{e}^{k})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosine</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosine</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R=\frac{1}{m}\sum\nolimits_{k=1}^{m}\max_{1\leq j\leq n}\mathrm{cosine}(y_{e}^{j},g_{e}^{k}),\quad P=\frac{1}{n}\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{n}\max_{1\leq k\leq m}\mathrm{cosine}(y_{e}^{j},g_{e}^{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Action-F1 is then obtained as the harmonic mean of precision and recall:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Action-F1}=2PR/(P+R)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Action-F1</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Action-F1}=2PR/(P+R),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Action-F1 provides an overall balance.
To explicitly evaluate the high-level semantic alignment, a core objective of our GSL, we utilize Perception Cosine Similarity (Perception-cos) and Reasoning Cosine Similarity (Reasoning-cos). These two metric compute the cosine similarity between the sentence embeddings of the generated text and the reference using a pretrained Sentence-BERT model, offering a robust measure of semantic cosine similarity in embedding space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Baselines.</span> We define the competitive group <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=\{A,B\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=\{A,B\}</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of a Learner (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>) and a Guide (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>) to represent models of relatively weaker and stronger capabilities, respectively. GCL is benchmarked against vanilla supervised fine-tuning (SFT), where baselines are established by independently fine-tuning each model under identical data configurations to ensure a controlled comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
All experiments are conducted on an 8<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> NVIDIA RTX8000 GPU cluster using DeepSpeed ZeRO-3 for memory efficiency. The optimizer is AdamW with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{sup}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{sup}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{GSL}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{GSL}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{DRL}=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{DRL}=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>.
To evaluate both robustness and performance limits, we report the following configurations.
For the SFT baseline, a fixed learning rate <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> is used, representing independent model optimization.
For ablation, symmetric DRL temperatures <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{A}=\tau_{B}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{A}=\tau_{B}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math> are adopted to create a challenging setting and verify the effectiveness of the loss functions under non-optimal optimization conditions.
Under GCL training, two models are used: a learner and a guide. For fair comparison, the learner uses <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and the guide <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=5\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=5\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We do not conduct physical robot experiments because our work focuses on the high-level planning layer of the navigation stack. In typical navigation systems, high-level planning, local trajectory generation, and low-level control are separated modules.
Our VLM operates at the planning stage, generating socially compliant navigation intentions from multimodal perception.
These decisions are executed by conventional local planners (e.g., DWA or TEB) and controllers producing velocity commands <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, this study mainly improves reasoning and planning capabilities, a prerequisite before integration with physical robot platforms.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of GCL across 12 competitive group.<span id="S4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{guide}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{guide}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{learner}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{learner}</annotation></semantics></math> are allocated to SFT baseline superiority. The performance hierarchy from lowest to highest is: Qwen2.5-VL-3B (0.692) <math id="S4.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-VL-8B (0.755) <math id="S4.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen2.5-VL-7B (0.777) <math id="S4.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-VL-2B (0.797) <math id="S4.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-VL-4B (0.816). Meanwhile, temperature parameters (<math id="S4.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>) are assigned solely based on parameter size (larger capacity = 2.0, smaller capacity = 3.0). <math id="S4.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes Action-F1. Bold text indicates optimal performance of the model.(</span>For MUSON results, details provided in the Supplementary Material<span id="S4.T1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:136.7pt;vertical-align:-66.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-103.6pt,41.1pt) scale(0.624772790250128,0.624772790250128) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.7.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.7.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regime A: Reshaping Mode</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Capacity<sub id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">L</sub> &lt; Capacity<sub id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.2.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_sub">G</sub>,  Small <math id="S4.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Large,  <math id="S4.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\eta_{learner}=1\times 10^{-5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>𝟏𝟎</mn><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">−</mo><mn>𝟓</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\eta_{learner}=1\times 10^{-5}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\eta_{guide}=5\times 10^{-6}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟓</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>𝟏𝟎</mn><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">−</mo><mn>𝟔</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\eta_{guide}=5\times 10^{-6}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.7.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Group (Learner / Guide)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tau_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>𝐋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tau_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S4.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tau_{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tau_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline (<math id="S4.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S4.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GCL (<math id="S4.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S4.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.7.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Qwen3-VL-2B / Qwen3-VL-4B</td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.0 / 2.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.797 / 0.816</td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.954</span> <span id="S4.T1.7.1.3.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">(+0.157)</span> / 0.910 <span id="S4.T1.7.1.3.4.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">(+0.094)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.7.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r">Qwen2.5-VL-3B / Qwen3-VL-4B</td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.0 / 2.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.692 / 0.816</td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.968</span> <span id="S4.T1.7.1.4.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">(+0.276)</span> / 0.914 <span id="S4.T1.7.1.4.4.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">(+0.098)</span></td></tr>
